                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In the 2000s, many European night-train services were cancelled because cheap flights had become more popular. Recently, however, overnight trains have made a comeback. In 2016, Austria's national railway company took over several routes that German operators had abandoned, and passenger numbers rose steadily. Supporters argue that night trains produce far fewer carbon emissions per passenger than planes, and they save travellers the cost of a hotel. Nevertheless, operators face challenges: sleeping cars are expensive to build, and night trains can carry fewer passengers than day trains because beds take up more space."
              }
            ]
